+// SK-LOCALRDV: process-local rendezvous for co-resident accounts.
+//
+// Several Jami accounts can live in ONE daemon (this fork runs four). Each is a separate
+// DhtRunner + ConnectionManager with its own device key, so to each other they are strangers:
+// the only identity check on the connect path is `deviceId == identity().second->getLongId()`,
+// which catches an account's OWN device and nothing else. Two accounts on the SAME PHONE
+// therefore rendezvous by putting an encrypted PeerConnectionRequest onto a DHT key — via a
+// public proxy — that is being listened to by a sibling object a few bytes away in the same
+// process. Measured 2026-07-31: 997 of 2861 device-id occurrences in an ICE census, i.e. ~35%
+// of all transports, were this phone talking to itself. Worse, each of those puts lands on a
+// `peer:` key with a 10-minute TTL that cannot be withdrawn, and every push about a new value
+// makes the owner re-download the WHOLE key (measured 148 KiB).
+//
+// This registry short-circuits ONLY the rendezvous. ICE, TLS, certificate verification and the
+// trust gate all still run exactly as before — nothing above dhtnet can tell the difference.
+// A miss falls through to the normal DHT put, so it is fail-safe by construction.
 class ConnectionManager::Impl : public std::enable_shared_from_this<ConnectionManager::Impl>
 {
 public:
+    // SK-LOCALRDV: the registry lives INSIDE Impl — at namespace scope `ConnectionManager::Impl`
+    // is private and cannot be named. weak_ptr to an incomplete type is well-formed here.
+    struct LocalRendezvous
+    {
+        std::mutex mtx;
+        std::map<DeviceId, std::weak_ptr<Impl>> byDevice;
+        static LocalRendezvous& instance()
+        {
+            static LocalRendezvous r;
+            return r;
+        }
+    };
+
